\section{Highlighting code chunks with minted}

This filter adds syntax highlighting to [[noweave]]'s LaTeX output by
typesetting code chunks with the \texttt{minted} package instead of
noweb's plain typewriter rendering.  Each chunk is highlighted
according to its own language, so a literate program that defines,
say, both a [[Makefile]] and a Python program gets each chunk
highlighted with the right lexer.

Typical use, as the \emph{last} filter on the command line:
\begin{verbatim}
noweave -autolang -autodefs python3 -index -filter tominted fib.nw > fib.tex
\end{verbatim}
The woven document must load the \texttt{minted} package, and it must
be compiled with shell escape enabled, e.g.\
[[pdflatex -shell-escape fib.tex]], because \texttt{minted} runs
Pygments at compile time.  This filter targets the LaTeX backend only;
do not combine it with [[-html]].

\subsection{Overview}

The filter is a Python~3 program that copies the noweb markup stream
from standard input to standard output, rewriting some code chunks.
It works in two passes over the (buffered) stream:

\begin{enumerate}
\item Determine the language of every code chunk.  This is the
      inference of the [[autolang]] filter---filename-like chunk
      names, shebang lines and lexical context seed the languages,
      which propagate along chunk uses---and it is not duplicated
      here: \texttt{autolang.nw} keeps it in the chunks
      [[inference constants]] and [[inference functions]], and this
      program is tangled from both files at once,
      \begin{verbatim}
notangle -Rtominted autolang.nw tominted.nw
      \end{verbatim}
      so those chunks are included below by reference.  An
      [[@language]] annotation already in the stream, put there by
      [[autolang]] upstream, is honored as authoritative; the
      inference merely fills in whatever an unannotated stream leaves
      open, so the filter also works in a pipeline without
      [[-autolang]].
\item Rewrite each code chunk whose language is known.  The chunk's
      [[@text]] lines are replaced by [[@literal]] lines that the
      LaTeX backend copies verbatim, wrapping the code in a
      [[minted]] environment.  Chunks of unknown language pass
      through untouched and keep noweb's classic rendering.
\end{enumerate}

Running last in the pipeline matters: after [[finduses]] and [[noidx]]
have run, all cross-reference labels exist in the stream, so we can
render chunk references inside highlighted code as real
\LA{}\dots\RA{} links with page tags (Section~\ref{sec:uses} below).  Had we
run earlier, converting [[@text]] to [[@literal]] would have hidden
the code from [[finduses]] and destroyed the identifier index.

The price of highlighting is that \emph{identifier} occurrences inside
highlighted code are no longer individually hyperlinked: Pygments owns
the code body, so noweb's sub-line markup cannot survive inside it.
The identifier index and the chunk cross-references still work,
because the [[@index]] and [[@xref]] anchors are not discarded but
relocated to just after the highlighted block, where they still
resolve to the same sub-page (Section~\ref{sec:anchors}).

Like [[autolang]], this is a Python~3 program with no dependency
beyond the standard library at filter time; Pygments is needed only
where the woven document is compiled, by \texttt{minted} itself.


\subsection{Program layout}

